       "content": {
        "id": "v2-proposal-j5-125015-root",
        "type": "question",
        "label": null,
        "prompt": "Find the Cartesian equation and describe the part of the graph that is traced.",
        "children": [
         {
          "id": "v2-proposal-j5-125015-j5-gp-a",
          "type": "part",
          "label": "a",
          "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}x&=t^2-2\\\\y&=t^2+1\\end{aligned}$$",
          "answerSpaceMm": 30,
          "answer": {
           "short": "$y=x+3$; the ray with closed endpoint $(-2,1)$ extending to the right ($x\\ge-2$, $y\\ge1$).",
           "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}t^2&=x+2\\\\y&=(x+2)+1\\\\y&=x+3\\end{aligned}$$\nSince $t^2\\ge0$, $x=t^2-2\\ge-2$ and $y=t^2+1\\ge1$. The graph is the ray on $y=x+3$ starting at $(-2,1)$ and extending to the right."
          }
         },
         {
          "id": "v2-proposal-j5-125015-j5-gp-b",
          "type": "part",
          "label": "b",
          "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}x&=5-t^2\\\\y&=2t^2\\end{aligned}$$",
          "answerSpaceMm": 30,
          "answer": {
           "short": "$2x+y=10$; the ray with closed endpoint $(5,0)$ extending to the left ($x\\le5$, $y\\ge0$).",
           "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}t^2&=5-x\\\\y&=2(5-x)\\\\y&=10-2x\\\\2x+y&=10\\end{aligned}$$\nSince $t^2\\ge0$, $x=5-t^2\\le5$ and $y=2t^2\\ge0$. The graph is the ray on $2x+y=10$ starting at $(5,0)$ and extending up to the left."
          }
         },
         {
          "id": "v2-proposal-j5-125015-j5-gp-c",
          "type": "part",
          "label": "c",
          "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}x&=2\\sin^2t\\\\y&=3\\cos^2t\\end{aligned}$$",
          "answerSpaceMm": 35,
          "answer": {
           "short": "$3x+2y=6$; the closed line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,0)$ ($0\\le x\\le2$, $0\\le y\\le3$).",
           "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\sin^2t&=\\frac{x}{2}\\\\\\cos^2t&=\\frac{y}{3}\\\\\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{y}{3}&=1\\\\3x+2y&=6\\end{aligned}$$\nSince $0\\le\\sin^2t\\le1$, $0\\le x\\le2$. Since $0\\le\\cos^2t\\le1$, $0\\le y\\le3$. The graph is the segment on $3x+2y=6$ joining $(0,3)$ and $(2,0)$, including both endpoints."
          }
         }
        ],
        "layout": "grid",
        "columns": 2
